var e={level:`A2`,type:`sentence-choice`,questions:[{id:`a2-sc-001`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`comparatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Short adjectives add -er in the comparative: taller than.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Short adjectives use -er, not 'more'.`,2:`'tallest' is a superlative. Use 'taller' with 'than'.`},options:[`She is taller than me.`,`She is more tall than me.`,`She is tallest than me.`],correct:0},{id:`a2-sc-002`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`going-to`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`For plans, use be + going to + base verb.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Go to visit ... now' mixes tenses and is not a normal sentence.`,2:`The words are in the wrong order. Use: I'm going to visit.`},options:[`I'm going to visit my aunt next week.`,`I go to visit my aunt next week now.`,`I visit going my aunt next week.`],correct:0},{id:`a2-sc-003`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`much-many`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use 'many' with countable nouns, and keep question order: How many people are there?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'people' is countable, so use 'many'.`,2:`In a question the verb comes before there: are there?`},options:[`How much people are there?`,`How many people are there?`,`How many people there are?`],correct:1},{id:`a2-sc-004`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Regular verbs add -ed (or -d): walk → walked. Example: Sam and I walked to the park last night.`,options:[`Sam and I walks to the park last night.`,`Sam and I walked to the park last night.`,`Sam and I walk to the park last night.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"walks" is the present simple. "last night" shows the past, so we use "walked".`,2:`"walk" is the base form. "last night" shows the past, so we use "walked".`}},{id:`a2-sc-005`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"see" is irregular: the past simple is "saw". Example: My friends saw a good film last night.`,options:[`My friends see a good film last night.`,`My friends seed a good film last night.`,`My friends saw a good film last night.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"see" is the base form. "last night" shows the past, so we use "saw".`,1:`"see" is irregular. The past is "saw", not "seed".`}},{id:`a2-sc-006`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Regular verbs add -ed (or -d): wash → washed. Example: Hugo washed the car in 2019.`,options:[`Hugo washed the car in 2019.`,`Hugo washes the car in 2019.`,`Hugo wash the car in 2019.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"washes" is the present simple. "in 2019" shows the past, so we use "washed".`,2:`"wash" is the base form. "in 2019" shows the past, so we use "washed".`}},{id:`a2-sc-007`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"come" is irregular: the past simple is "came". Example: Anna came to my party this morning.`,options:[`Anna came to my party this morning.`,`Anna comed to my party this morning.`,`Anna was come to my party this morning.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"come" is irregular. The past is "came", not "comed".`,2:`"was" cannot go before the base verb "come". For a finished action we use "came".`}},{id:`a2-sc-008`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"swim" is irregular: the past simple is "swam". Example: He swam in the sea yesterday.`,options:[`He swam in the sea yesterday.`,`He swims in the sea yesterday.`,`He swimed in the sea yesterday.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"swims" is the present simple. "yesterday" shows the past, so we use "swam".`,2:`"swim" is irregular. The past is "swam", not "swimed".`}},{id:`a2-sc-009`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Regular verbs add -ed (or -d): dance → danced. Example: We danced at the party last night.`,options:[`We danced at the party last night.`,`We dances at the party last night.`,`We danceed at the party last night.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"dances" is the present simple. "last night" shows the past, so we use "danced".`,2:`Verbs ending in -e just add -d: danced.`}},{id:`a2-sc-010`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Regular verbs add -ed (or -d): plan → planned. Example: Our teacher planned a trip this morning.`,options:[`Our teacher planed a trip this morning.`,`Our teacher planned a trip this morning.`,`Our teacher was plan a trip this morning.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`Short verbs ending in one vowel + one consonant double the last letter: planned.`,2:`"was" cannot go before the base verb "plan". For a finished action we use "planned".`}},{id:`a2-sc-011`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"draw" is irregular: the past simple is "drew". Example: Anna drew a picture last weekend.`,options:[`Anna drew a picture last weekend.`,`Anna drawed a picture last weekend.`,`Anna draw a picture last weekend.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"draw" is irregular. The past is "drew", not "drawed".`,2:`"draw" is the base form. "last weekend" shows the past, so we use "drew".`}},{id:`a2-sc-012`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-negative`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`After Did use the base verb: Did Sara make a cake?`,options:[`Did Sara make a cake last year?`,`Did Sara made a cake last year?`,`Did Sara to make a cake last year?`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`After "Did" the verb stays in the base form: make, not "made".`,2:`After "Did" we do not use "to". We use the base form: make.`}},{id:`a2-sc-013`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-negative`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`After Did use the base verb: Did the children sing a song?`,options:[`Did the children sings a song on Monday?`,`Did the children to sing a song on Monday?`,`Did the children sing a song on Monday?`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`After "Did" the verb has no -s: sing.`,1:`After "Did" we do not use "to". We use the base form: sing.`}},{id:`a2-sc-014`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-negative`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Past questions use Did + subject + base verb: Did Leo like the film?`,options:[`Does Leo like the film on Monday?`,`Did Leo like the film on Monday?`,`Do Leo like the film on Monday?`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Does" is the present. "on Monday" shows the past, so we use "Did".`,2:`"Do" is the present and does not agree with "Leo". For the past we use "Did".`}},{id:`a2-sc-015`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-negative`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In the past negative use didn't + base verb: They didn't love the song.`,options:[`They didn't love the song last week.`,`They don't love the song last week.`,`They not love the song last week.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"don't" is the present. "last week" shows the past, so we use "didn't".`,2:`"not" cannot go directly before "love". In the past we use "didn't".`}},{id:`a2-sc-016`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-negative`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`After Did use the base verb: Did the girls like the film?`,options:[`Did the girls like the film last year?`,`Did the girls likes the film last year?`,`Did the girls liked the film last year?`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`After "Did" the verb has no -s: like.`,2:`After "Did" the verb stays in the base form: like, not "liked".`}},{id:`a2-sc-017`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-negative`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`After didn't use the base verb: Ben and Lucy didn't tell a funny story.`,options:[`Ben and Lucy didn't tell a funny story this morning.`,`Ben and Lucy didn't to tell a funny story this morning.`,`Ben and Lucy didn't told a funny story this morning.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`After "didn't" we do not use "to". We use the base form: tell.`,2:`After "didn't" the verb stays in the base form: tell, not "told".`}},{id:`a2-sc-018`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-negative`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In the past negative use didn't + base verb: The children didn't draw a picture.`,options:[`The children don't draw a picture last night.`,`The children didn't draw a picture last night.`,`The children weren't draw a picture last night.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"don't" is the present. "last night" shows the past, so we use "didn't".`,2:`"weren't" is used with "be", not before the base verb "draw". We use "didn't".`}},{id:`a2-sc-019`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-negative`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In the past negative use didn't + base verb: Nina didn't come to my party.`,options:[`Nina didn't come to my party in 2019.`,`Nina wasn't come to my party in 2019.`,`Nina no come to my party in 2019.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"wasn't" is used with "be", not before the base verb "come". We use "didn't".`,2:`"no" cannot make a verb negative. We use "didn't".`}},{id:`a2-sc-020`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-negative`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`After didn't use the base verb: Zoe didn't paint the wall.`,options:[`Zoe didn't paint the wall this morning.`,`Zoe didn't paints the wall this morning.`,`Zoe didn't to paint the wall this morning.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`After "didn't" the verb has no -s: paint.`,2:`After "didn't" we do not use "to". We use the base form: paint.`}},{id:`a2-sc-021`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`past-negative`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In the past negative use didn't + base verb: Nina didn't walk to the park.`,options:[`Nina doesn't walk to the park this morning.`,`Nina not walk to the park this morning.`,`Nina didn't walk to the park this morning.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"doesn't" is the present. "this morning" shows the past, so we use "didn't".`,1:`"not" cannot go directly before "walk". In the past we use "didn't".`}},{id:`a2-sc-022`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`was-were`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In questions "was/were" comes before the subject: Was Mark in the garden last night?`,options:[`Was Mark in the garden last night?`,`Did Mark in the garden last night?`,`Were Mark in the garden last night?`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Did" is used before a base verb, not with "be". We say "Was Mark...?".`,2:`"Were" is used with you, we, they and more than one person or thing. With "Mark" we use "was".`}},{id:`a2-sc-023`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`was-were`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The past of "be": was with I, he, she, it; were with you, we, they. Example: I was in the park last night.`,options:[`I were in the park last night.`,`I was in the park last night.`,`I be in the park last night.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"were" is used with you, we, they and more than one person or thing. With "I" we use "was".`,2:`"be" alone cannot be the verb here. In the past we use "was".`}},{id:`a2-sc-024`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`was-were`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The negative of "was/were" is "wasn't/weren't": Nina wasn't at the cinema this morning.`,options:[`Nina didn't at the cinema this morning.`,`Nina weren't at the cinema this morning.`,`Nina wasn't at the cinema this morning.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"didn't" is used before a base verb, not with "be". We say "wasn't".`,1:`"weren't" is used with you, we, they and more than one person or thing. With "Nina" we use "was".`}},{id:`a2-sc-025`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`was-were`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In questions "was/were" comes before the subject: Was Sara very busy this morning?`,options:[`Was Sara very busy this morning?`,`Did Sara very busy this morning?`,`Is Sara very busy this morning?`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Did" is used before a base verb, not with "be". We say "Was Sara...?".`,2:`"Is" is the present. "this morning" shows the past, so we use "Was".`}},{id:`a2-sc-026`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`was-were`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The negative of "was/were" is "wasn't/weren't": Tom wasn't in the park last summer.`,options:[`Tom wasn't in the park last summer.`,`Tom isn't in the park last summer.`,`Tom weren't in the park last summer.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"isn't" is the present. "last summer" shows the past, so we use "wasn't".`,2:`"weren't" is used with you, we, they and more than one person or thing. With "Tom" we use "was".`}},{id:`a2-sc-027`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`was-were`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The negative of "was/were" is "wasn't/weren't": Kate wasn't ready two days ago.`,options:[`Kate weren't ready two days ago.`,`Kate isn't ready two days ago.`,`Kate wasn't ready two days ago.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"weren't" is used with you, we, they and more than one person or thing. With "Kate" we use "was".`,1:`"isn't" is the present. "two days ago" shows the past, so we use "wasn't".`}},{id:`a2-sc-028`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`was-were`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The past of "be": was with I, he, she, it; were with you, we, they. Example: She was happy yesterday.`,options:[`She were happy yesterday.`,`She was happy yesterday.`,`She been happy yesterday.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"were" is used with you, we, they and more than one person or thing. With "she" we use "was".`,2:`"been" needs "have" or "has" before it. For a simple past sentence we use "was".`}},{id:`a2-sc-029`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`was-were`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The past of "be": was with I, he, she, it; were with you, we, they. Example: He was in the garden two days ago.`,options:[`He is in the garden two days ago.`,`He were in the garden two days ago.`,`He was in the garden two days ago.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"is" is the present of "be". "two days ago" shows the past, so we use "was".`,1:`"were" is used with you, we, they and more than one person or thing. With "he" we use "was".`}},{id:`a2-sc-030`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`was-were`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In questions "was/were" comes before the subject: Was Rosa in London two days ago?`,options:[`Was Rosa in London two days ago?`,`Did Rosa in London two days ago?`,`Were Rosa in London two days ago?`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Did" is used before a base verb, not with "be". We say "Was Rosa...?".`,2:`"Were" is used with you, we, they and more than one person or thing. With "Rosa" we use "was".`}},{id:`a2-sc-031`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`was-were`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In questions "was/were" comes before the subject: Was Mark ill two days ago?`,options:[`Is Mark ill two days ago?`,`Were Mark ill two days ago?`,`Was Mark ill two days ago?`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Is" is the present. "two days ago" shows the past, so we use "Was".`,1:`"Were" is used with you, we, they and more than one person or thing. With "Mark" we use "was".`}},{id:`a2-sc-032`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`was-were`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The past of "be": was with I, he, she, it; were with you, we, they. Example: Jack was at work this morning.`,options:[`Jack was at work this morning.`,`Jack be at work this morning.`,`Jack were at work this morning.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"be" alone cannot be the verb here. In the past we use "was".`,2:`"were" is used with you, we, they and more than one person or thing. With "Jack" we use "was".`}},{id:`a2-sc-033`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`was-were`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The negative of "was/were" is "wasn't/weren't": We weren't happy this morning.`,options:[`We didn't happy this morning.`,`We weren't happy this morning.`,`We aren't happy this morning.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"didn't" is used before a base verb, not with "be". We say "weren't".`,2:`"aren't" is the present. "this morning" shows the past, so we use "weren't".`}},{id:`a2-sc-034`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`was-were`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`In questions "was/were" comes before the subject: Was Leo at home last night?`,options:[`Were Leo at home last night?`,`Is Leo at home last night?`,`Was Leo at home last night?`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Were" is used with you, we, they and more than one person or thing. With "Leo" we use "was".`,1:`"Is" is the present. "last night" shows the past, so we use "Was".`}},{id:`a2-sc-035`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`comparatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Short adjectives add -er: shorter than. Example: Ben is shorter than Kate.`,options:[`Ben is more short than Kate.`,`Ben is short than Kate.`,`Ben is shorter than Kate.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"short" is a short adjective. Short adjectives add -er, not "more": shorter.`,1:`We compare two things, so the adjective needs the comparative form: "shorter" than.`}},{id:`a2-sc-036`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`comparatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Long adjectives use "more": more expensive than. Example: The red car is more expensive than the blue car.`,options:[`The red car is more expensive than the blue car.`,`The red car is expensive than the blue car.`,`The red car is expensiveer than the blue car.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`We compare two things, so the adjective needs the comparative form: "more expensive" than.`,2:`"expensive" is a long adjective. Long adjectives use "more": more expensive.`}},{id:`a2-sc-037`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`comparatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Long adjectives use "more": more famous than. Example: This shop is more famous than that shop.`,options:[`This shop is famous than that shop.`,`This shop is more famous than that shop.`,`This shop is famouser than that shop.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`We compare two things, so the adjective needs the comparative form: "more famous" than.`,2:`"famous" is a long adjective. Long adjectives use "more": more famous.`}},{id:`a2-sc-038`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`comparatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Long adjectives use "more": more boring than. Example: Lily is more boring than Ivan.`,options:[`Lily is boring than Ivan.`,`Lily is more boring than Ivan.`,`Lily is most boring than Ivan.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`We compare two things, so the adjective needs the comparative form: "more boring" than.`,2:`"most boring" is the superlative, used with "the" for three or more things. For two things use "more boring".`}},{id:`a2-sc-039`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`comparatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Double the last consonant and add -er: thinner than. Example: Mia is thinner than Sam.`,options:[`Mia is thinner than Sam.`,`Mia is thinnest than Sam.`,`Mia is thin than Sam.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"thinnest" is the superlative, used with "the" for three or more things. For two things use "thinner".`,2:`We compare two things, so the adjective needs the comparative form: "thinner" than.`}},{id:`a2-sc-040`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`comparatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Short adjectives add -er: smaller than. Example: This film is smaller than that film.`,options:[`This film is smallest than that film.`,`This film is small than that film.`,`This film is smaller than that film.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"smallest" is the superlative, used with "the" for three or more things. For two things use "smaller".`,1:`We compare two things, so the adjective needs the comparative form: "smaller" than.`}},{id:`a2-sc-041`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`comparatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Double the last consonant and add -er: bigger than. Example: Ravi is bigger than Sam.`,options:[`Ravi is bigger than Sam.`,`Ravi is biger than Sam.`,`Ravi is more big than Sam.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`Short adjectives ending in one vowel + one consonant double the consonant: bigger.`,2:`"big" is a short adjective. Short adjectives add -er, not "more": bigger.`}},{id:`a2-sc-042`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`comparatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Change -y to -ier: heavier than. Example: This road is heavier than that road.`,options:[`This road is heaviest than that road.`,`This road is heavier than that road.`,`This road is heavy than that road.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"heaviest" is the superlative, used with "the" for three or more things. For two things use "heavier".`,2:`We compare two things, so the adjective needs the comparative form: "heavier" than.`}},{id:`a2-sc-043`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`superlatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Change -y to -iest: the happiest. Use "the" + superlative for the best of three or more.`,options:[`Rosa is the most happy girl in the school.`,`Rosa is the happiest girl in the school.`,`Rosa is the happy girl in the school.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"happy" is a short adjective. Short adjectives add -est, not "most": happiest.`,2:`After "the" for the best of a group we need the superlative: "happiest".`}},{id:`a2-sc-044`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`superlatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Change -y to -iest: the busiest. Use "the" + superlative for the best of three or more.`,options:[`Kate is the busiest girl in the group.`,`Kate is the busyest girl in the group.`,`Kate is the busier girl in the group.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`After a consonant + y, change y to -iest: busiest.`,2:`"busier" is the comparative, used with "than". For the best of a group use "the busiest".`}},{id:`a2-sc-045`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`superlatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Long adjectives use "most": the most dangerous. Use "the" + superlative for the best of three or more.`,options:[`This is the most dangerous hotel in the town.`,`This is the dangerous hotel in the town.`,`This is the more dangerous hotel in the town.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`After "the" for the best of a group we need the superlative: "most dangerous".`,2:`"more dangerous" is the comparative, used with "than". For the best of a group use "the most dangerous".`}},{id:`a2-sc-046`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`superlatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Short adjectives add -est: the kindest. Use "the" + superlative for the best of three or more.`,options:[`Leo is the kindest boy in the team.`,`Leo is the kinder boy in the team.`,`Leo is the kind boy in the team.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"kinder" is the comparative, used with "than". For the best of a group use "the kindest".`,2:`After "the" for the best of a group we need the superlative: "kindest".`}},{id:`a2-sc-047`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`superlatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Short adjectives add -est: the cleanest. Use "the" + superlative for the best of three or more.`,options:[`This is the cleaner hotel in the village.`,`This is the cleanest hotel in the village.`,`This is the most clean hotel in the village.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"cleaner" is the comparative, used with "than". For the best of a group use "the cleanest".`,2:`"clean" is a short adjective. Short adjectives add -est, not "most": cleanest.`}},{id:`a2-sc-048`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`superlatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Short adjectives add -est: the newest. Use "the" + superlative for the best of three or more.`,options:[`This is the newest café in the village.`,`This is the new café in the village.`,`This is the newer café in the village.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`After "the" for the best of a group we need the superlative: "newest".`,2:`"newer" is the comparative, used with "than". For the best of a group use "the newest".`}},{id:`a2-sc-049`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`superlatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Long adjectives use "most": the most popular. Use "the" + superlative for the best of three or more.`,options:[`This is the popularest hotel in the city.`,`This is the most popular hotel in the city.`,`This is the popular hotel in the city.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"popular" is a long adjective. Long adjectives use "most": most popular.`,2:`After "the" for the best of a group we need the superlative: "most popular".`}},{id:`a2-sc-050`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`superlatives`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Short adjectives add -est: the tallest. Use "the" + superlative for the best of three or more.`,options:[`Mark is the tall boy in the group.`,`Mark is the most tall boy in the group.`,`Mark is the tallest boy in the group.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`After "the" for the best of a group we need the superlative: "tallest".`,1:`"tall" is a short adjective. Short adjectives add -est, not "most": tallest.`}},{id:`a2-sc-051`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`going-to`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use be + going to + base verb for plans: My sister is going to cook rice tomorrow.`,options:[`My sister is going to cook rice tomorrow.`,`My sister is go to cook rice tomorrow.`,`My sister are going to cook rice tomorrow.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`We use "going to" for plans, not "go to": is going to cook.`,2:`With "my sister" we use "is", not "are": is going to.`}},{id:`a2-sc-052`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`going-to`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use be + going to + base verb for plans: The girls are going to make a cake next year.`,options:[`The girls is going to make a cake next year.`,`The girls are going make a cake next year.`,`The girls are going to make a cake next year.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`With "the girls" we use "are", not "is": are going to.`,1:`After "going" we need "to" before the verb: are going to make.`}},{id:`a2-sc-053`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`going-to`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use be + going to + base verb for plans: Paul is going to jump on the bed tomorrow.`,options:[`Paul is going jump on the bed tomorrow.`,`Paul goes to jump on the bed tomorrow.`,`Paul is going to jump on the bed tomorrow.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`After "going" we need "to" before the verb: is going to jump.`,1:`A plan needs "be + going to". "goes to" is not the future form.`}},{id:`a2-sc-054`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`going-to`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use be + going to + base verb for plans: Emma is going to help the teacher this weekend.`,options:[`Emma am going to help the teacher this weekend.`,`Emma goes to help the teacher this weekend.`,`Emma is going to help the teacher this weekend.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`With "Emma" we use "is", not "am": is going to.`,1:`A plan needs "be + going to". "goes to" is not the future form.`}},{id:`a2-sc-055`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`going-to`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use be + going to + base verb for plans: Nina is going to visit a friend next year.`,options:[`Nina am going to visit a friend next year.`,`Nina is going to visit a friend next year.`,`Nina is going visit a friend next year.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`With "Nina" we use "is", not "am": is going to.`,2:`After "going" we need "to" before the verb: is going to visit.`}},{id:`a2-sc-056`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`going-to`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use be + going to + base verb for plans: Jack is going to walk to work tonight.`,options:[`Jack is going walk to work tonight.`,`Jack is go to walk to work tonight.`,`Jack is going to walk to work tonight.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`After "going" we need "to" before the verb: is going to walk.`,1:`We use "going to" for plans, not "go to": is going to walk.`}},{id:`a2-sc-057`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`going-to`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use be + going to + base verb for plans: Leo is going to eat breakfast this evening.`,options:[`Leo is going to eat breakfast this evening.`,`Leo is go to eat breakfast this evening.`,`Leo goes to eat breakfast this evening.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`We use "going to" for plans, not "go to": is going to eat.`,2:`A plan needs "be + going to". "goes to" is not the future form.`}},{id:`a2-sc-058`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`going-to`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use be + going to + base verb for plans: Mark is going to carry a heavy bag next year.`,options:[`Mark am going to carry a heavy bag next year.`,`Mark is going to carry a heavy bag next year.`,`Mark is going carry a heavy bag next year.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`With "Mark" we use "is", not "am": is going to.`,2:`After "going" we need "to" before the verb: is going to carry.`}},{id:`a2-sc-059`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`going-to`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use be + going to + base verb for plans: They are going to learn French tomorrow.`,options:[`They are going to learn French tomorrow.`,`They go to learn French tomorrow.`,`They are going learn French tomorrow.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`A plan needs "be + going to". "go to" is not the future form.`,2:`After "going" we need "to" before the verb: are going to learn.`}},{id:`a2-sc-060`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`much-many`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "a little" for a small amount of an uncountable noun: a little rain.`,options:[`We have only many rain.`,`We have only much rain.`,`We have only a little rain.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"many" is used with countable nouns. "rain" is uncountable.`,1:`"much" does not go after "only". For a small amount we say "a little".`}},{id:`a2-sc-061`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`much-many`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "many" with plural countable nouns in questions: How many flowers?`,options:[`How many flowers do you need?`,`How much flowers do you need?`,`How a little flowers do you need?`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"much" is used with uncountable nouns. "flowers" is countable.`,2:`"a little" is used with uncountable nouns. "flowers" is countable.`}},{id:`a2-sc-062`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`much-many`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "too much" with uncountable nouns: too much traffic.`,options:[`There is too many traffic in the soup.`,`There is too a few traffic in the soup.`,`There is too much traffic in the soup.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"many" is used with countable nouns. "traffic" is uncountable.`,1:`"a few" is used with plural countable nouns, not with "traffic".`}},{id:`a2-sc-063`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`much-many`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "too much" with uncountable nouns: too much snow.`,options:[`There is too much snow in the soup.`,`There is too a snow in the soup.`,`There is too many snow in the soup.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"a" cannot be used with an uncountable noun like "snow".`,2:`"many" is used with countable nouns. "snow" is uncountable.`}},{id:`a2-sc-064`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`much-many`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "a little" for a small amount of an uncountable noun: a little snow.`,options:[`We have only a little snow.`,`We have only much snow.`,`We have only many snow.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"much" does not go after "only". For a small amount we say "a little".`,2:`"many" is used with countable nouns. "snow" is uncountable.`}},{id:`a2-sc-065`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`much-many`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "many" with plural countable nouns in questions: How many students?`,options:[`How a little students do you need?`,`How many students do you need?`,`How a students do you need?`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"a little" is used with uncountable nouns. "students" is countable.`,2:`"a" is used with one thing. "students" is plural.`}},{id:`a2-sc-066`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`much-many`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "much" with uncountable nouns in questions: How much music?`,options:[`How much music do you need?`,`How a few music do you need?`,`How many music do you need?`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"a few" is used with plural countable nouns, not with "music".`,2:`"many" is used with countable nouns. "music" is uncountable.`}},{id:`a2-sc-067`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`much-many`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "too much" with uncountable nouns: too much pasta.`,options:[`There is too a pasta in the soup.`,`There is too many pasta in the soup.`,`There is too much pasta in the soup.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"a" cannot be used with an uncountable noun like "pasta".`,1:`"many" is used with countable nouns. "pasta" is uncountable.`}},{id:`a2-sc-068`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`much-many`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "much" with uncountable nouns in questions: How much salt?`,options:[`How many salt do you need?`,`How much salt do you need?`,`How a salt do you need?`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"many" is used with countable nouns. "salt" is uncountable.`,2:`"a" is used with one countable thing. "salt" is uncountable.`}},{id:`a2-sc-069`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`much-many`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "many" with plural countable nouns in questions: How many cakes?`,options:[`How much cakes do you need?`,`How a cakes do you need?`,`How many cakes do you need?`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"much" is used with uncountable nouns. "cakes" is countable.`,1:`"a" is used with one thing. "cakes" is plural.`}},{id:`a2-sc-070`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`some-any`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "some" in offers and requests: Would you like some photos?`,options:[`Would you like a photos?`,`Would you like some photos?`,`Would you like any photos?`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"a" is used with one countable thing, but "photos" is plural.`,2:`"any" is not used in offers. We say "Would you like some...?"`}},{id:`a2-sc-071`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`some-any`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "some" in offers and requests: Would you like some soup?`,options:[`Would you like any soup?`,`Would you like some soup?`,`Would you like a soup?`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"any" is not used in offers. We say "Would you like some...?"`,2:`"a" cannot be used with an uncountable noun like "soup".`}},{id:`a2-sc-072`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`some-any`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "any" in questions: Is there any money in the fridge?`,options:[`Is there some money in the fridge?`,`Is there a money in the fridge?`,`Is there any money in the fridge?`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"some" is not usual in questions. We use "any" to ask if something exists.`,1:`"a" cannot be used with an uncountable noun like "money".`}},{id:`a2-sc-073`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`some-any`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "any" in negative sentences: There aren't any cakes in my bag.`,options:[`There aren't some cakes in my bag.`,`There aren't a cakes in my bag.`,`There aren't any cakes in my bag.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"some" is normally used in positive sentences. In negatives we use "any".`,1:`"a" is used with one countable thing, but "cakes" is plural.`}},{id:`a2-sc-074`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`some-any`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "any" in questions: Is there any fruit in the fridge?`,options:[`Is there any fruit in the fridge?`,`Is there some fruit in the fridge?`,`Is there a fruit in the fridge?`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"some" is not usual in questions. We use "any" to ask if something exists.`,2:`"a" cannot be used with an uncountable noun like "fruit".`}},{id:`a2-sc-075`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`some-any`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "any" in negative sentences: There aren't any apples on the table.`,options:[`There aren't some apples on the table.`,`There aren't a apples on the table.`,`There aren't any apples on the table.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"some" is normally used in positive sentences. In negatives we use "any".`,1:`"a" is used with one countable thing, but "apples" is plural.`}},{id:`a2-sc-076`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`some-any`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "any" in questions: Is there any sugar in the fridge?`,options:[`Is there any sugar in the fridge?`,`Is there some sugar in the fridge?`,`Is there a sugar in the fridge?`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"some" is not usual in questions. We use "any" to ask if something exists.`,2:`"a" cannot be used with an uncountable noun like "sugar".`}},{id:`a2-sc-077`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`some-any`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "any" in questions: Is there any pasta on the table?`,options:[`Is there some pasta on the table?`,`Is there a pasta on the table?`,`Is there any pasta on the table?`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"some" is not usual in questions. We use "any" to ask if something exists.`,1:`"a" cannot be used with an uncountable noun like "pasta".`}},{id:`a2-sc-078`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`some-any`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "some" in positive sentences: There is some coffee in my bag.`,options:[`There is any coffee in my bag.`,`There is a coffee in my bag.`,`There is some coffee in my bag.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"any" is normally used in negatives and questions. This is a positive sentence, so we use "some".`,1:`"a" cannot be used with an uncountable noun like "coffee".`}},{id:`a2-sc-079`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`some-any`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "some" in positive sentences: There are some friends on the shelf.`,options:[`There are any friends on the shelf.`,`There are a friends on the shelf.`,`There are some friends on the shelf.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"any" is normally used in negatives and questions. This is a positive sentence, so we use "some".`,1:`"a" is used with one countable thing, but "friends" is plural.`}},{id:`a2-sc-080`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "don't have to" for something that is not necessary: Tom don't have to pay for the ticket. It is free for children.`,options:[`Tom can pay for the ticket. It is free for children.`,`Tom don't have to pay for the ticket. It is free for children.`,`Tom mustn't pay for the ticket. It is free for children.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"can" is used for ability or permission, but this sentence is about something not necessary.`,2:`"mustn't" is used for something that is forbidden, but this sentence is about something not necessary.`}},{id:`a2-sc-081`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "can" for ability or permission: Anna can speak three languages. She is very clever.`,options:[`Anna should speak three languages. She is very clever.`,`Anna can speak three languages. She is very clever.`,`Anna must speak three languages. She is very clever.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"should" is used for advice, but this sentence is about ability.`,2:`"must" is used for a strong rule or need, but this sentence is about ability.`}},{id:`a2-sc-082`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "shouldn't" for advice against something: He shouldn't drive so fast. It is not a good idea.`,options:[`He should drive so fast. It is not a good idea.`,`He have to drive so fast. It is not a good idea.`,`He shouldn't drive so fast. It is not a good idea.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"should" is used for advice, but this sentence is about advice against something.`,1:`"have to" is used for something that is necessary, but this sentence is about advice against something.`}},{id:`a2-sc-083`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "don't have to" for something that is not necessary: You don't have to bring a pen. We have lots of pens here.`,options:[`You can't bring a pen. We have lots of pens here.`,`You have to bring a pen. We have lots of pens here.`,`You don't have to bring a pen. We have lots of pens here.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"can't" is used for inability or something that is not allowed, but this sentence is about something not necessary.`,1:`"have to" is used for something that is necessary, but this sentence is about something not necessary.`}},{id:`a2-sc-084`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "mustn't" for something that is forbidden: Children mustn't play near the river. It is dangerous and forbidden.`,options:[`Children must play near the river. It is dangerous and forbidden.`,`Children don't have to play near the river. It is dangerous and forbidden.`,`Children mustn't play near the river. It is dangerous and forbidden.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"must" is used for a strong rule or need, but this sentence is about something forbidden.`,1:`"don't have to" is used for something that is not necessary, but this sentence is about something forbidden.`}},{id:`a2-sc-085`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "must" (or "have to") for a strong rule or need: Students must wear a uniform at our school. It is the rule.`,options:[`Students must wear a uniform at our school. It is the rule.`,`Students should wear a uniform at our school. It is the rule.`,`Students mustn't wear a uniform at our school. It is the rule.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"should" is used for advice, but this sentence is about a rule or something necessary.`,2:`"mustn't" is used for something that is forbidden, but this sentence is about a rule or something necessary.`}},{id:`a2-sc-086`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "mustn't" for something that is forbidden: You mustn't smoke in the hospital. It is against the rules.`,options:[`You mustn't smoke in the hospital. It is against the rules.`,`You can't smoke in the hospital. It is against the rules.`,`You can smoke in the hospital. It is against the rules.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"can't" is used for inability or something that is not allowed, but this sentence is about something forbidden.`,2:`"can" is used for ability or permission, but this sentence is about something forbidden.`}},{id:`a2-sc-087`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "must" (or "have to") for a strong rule or need: I must finish my homework tonight. It is compulsory.`,options:[`I should finish my homework tonight. It is compulsory.`,`I can't finish my homework tonight. It is compulsory.`,`I must finish my homework tonight. It is compulsory.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"should" is used for advice, but this sentence is about a rule or something necessary.`,1:`"can't" is used for inability or something that is not allowed, but this sentence is about a rule or something necessary.`}},{id:`a2-sc-088`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`prepositions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "during" for something that is happening (during the film): the nighthey ate popcorn during T.`,options:[`the nighthey ate popcorn ago T.`,`the nighthey ate popcorn for T.`,`the nighthey ate popcorn during T.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"ago" is used for a time back from now (two days ago). Here we need "during" (something that is happening (during the film)).`,1:`"for" is used for a length of time (for two hours). Here we need "during" (something that is happening (during the film)).`}},{id:`a2-sc-089`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`prepositions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "during" for something that is happening (during the film): He talked during the holidays.`,options:[`He talked for the holidays.`,`He talked during the holidays.`,`He talked since the holidays.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"for" is used for a length of time (for two hours). Here we need "during" (something that is happening (during the film)).`,2:`"since" is used for a starting point in time (since Monday). Here we need "during" (something that is happening (during the film)).`}},{id:`a2-sc-090`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`prepositions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"down" means to a lower place on something: My father ran down the hill.`,options:[`My father ran into the hill.`,`My father ran down the hill.`,`My father ran up the hill.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"into" means from outside to inside something, not "to a lower place on".`,2:`"up" means to a higher place on something, not "to a lower place on".`}},{id:`a2-sc-091`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`prepositions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"up" means to a higher place on something: My father ran up the mountain.`,options:[`My father ran down the mountain.`,`My father ran up the mountain.`,`My father ran through the mountain.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"down" means to a lower place on something, not "to a higher place on".`,2:`"through" means in one side and out of the other side of something, not "to a higher place on".`}},{id:`a2-sc-092`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`prepositions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "ago" for a time back from now (two days ago): She called me a week ago.`,options:[`She called me a week for.`,`She called me a week during.`,`She called me a week ago.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"for" is used for a length of time (for two hours). Here we need "ago" (a time back from now (two days ago)).`,1:`"during" is used for something that is happening (during the film). Here we need "ago" (a time back from now (two days ago)).`}},{id:`a2-sc-093`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`prepositions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"along" means in the same direction as something: The dog ran along the river.`,options:[`The dog ran up the river.`,`The dog ran along the river.`,`The dog ran across the river.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"up" means to a higher place on something, not "in the same direction as".`,2:`"across" means from one side to the other side of something, not "in the same direction as".`}},{id:`a2-sc-094`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`prepositions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"into" means from outside to inside something: The dog walked into the room.`,options:[`The dog walked into the room.`,`The dog walked out of the room.`,`The dog walked along the room.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"out of" means from inside to outside something, not "from outside to inside".`,2:`"along" means in the same direction as something, not "from outside to inside".`}},{id:`a2-sc-095`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`prepositions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "for" for a length of time (for two hours): He worked there for ten minutes.`,options:[`He worked there ago ten minutes.`,`He worked there for ten minutes.`,`He worked there since ten minutes.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"ago" is used for a time back from now (two days ago). Here we need "for" (a length of time (for two hours)).`,2:`"since" is used for a starting point in time (since Monday). Here we need "for" (a length of time (for two hours)).`}},{id:`a2-sc-096`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`prepositions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"down" means to a lower place on something: The boys walked down the stairs.`,options:[`The boys walked through the stairs.`,`The boys walked along the stairs.`,`The boys walked down the stairs.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"through" means in one side and out of the other side of something, not "to a lower place on".`,1:`"along" means in the same direction as something, not "to a lower place on".`}},{id:`a2-sc-097`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`prepositions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Use "ago" for a time back from now (two days ago): a monthhey moved here T ago.`,options:[`a monthhey moved here T ago.`,`a monthhey moved here T for.`,`a monthhey moved here T since.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"for" is used for a length of time (for two hours). Here we need "ago" (a time back from now (two days ago)).`,2:`"since" is used for a starting point in time (since Monday). Here we need "ago" (a time back from now (two days ago)).`}},{id:`a2-sc-098`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`prepositions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"along" means in the same direction as something: The dog ran along the river.`,options:[`The dog ran along the river.`,`The dog ran through the river.`,`The dog ran up the river.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"through" means in one side and out of the other side of something, not "in the same direction as".`,2:`"up" means to a higher place on something, not "in the same direction as".`}},{id:`a2-sc-099`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`prepositions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"along" means in the same direction as something: The dog ran along the beach.`,options:[`The dog ran along the beach.`,`The dog ran out of the beach.`,`The dog ran into the beach.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"out of" means from inside to outside something, not "in the same direction as".`,2:`"into" means from outside to inside something, not "in the same direction as".`}},{id:`a2-sc-100`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`With he, she, it and one person or thing, the verb takes -s/-es/-ies: Eva likes.`,options:[`Eva like cats.`,`Eva is like cats.`,`Eva likes cats.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"like" is the base form. It is used with I, you, we, they and plural subjects, not with "Eva".`,1:`"is" cannot be used before the base verb "like". Use "likes" alone.`}},{id:`a2-sc-101`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`With he, she, it and one person or thing, the verb takes -s/-es/-ies: Tom eats.`,options:[`Tom eats an apple.`,`Tom eating an apple.`,`Tom eat an apple.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"eating" needs "is" before it. For a routine use "eats".`,2:`"eat" is the base form. It is used with I, you, we, they and plural subjects, not with "Tom".`}},{id:`a2-sc-102`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`With he, she, it and one person or thing, the verb takes -s/-es/-ies: Ivan teaches.`,options:[`Ivan teaching music.`,`Ivan teaches music.`,`Ivan is teach music.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"teaching" needs "is" before it. For a routine use "teaches".`,2:`"is" cannot be used before the base verb "teach". Use "teaches" alone.`}},{id:`a2-sc-103`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`With I, you, we, they and plural subjects we use the base form: Sam and I carry.`,options:[`Sam and I carry a heavy bag.`,`Sam and I carries a heavy bag.`,`Sam and I are carry a heavy bag.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"carries" is for he, she, it and one person or thing. With "Sam and I" we use the base form "carry".`,2:`"are" cannot be used before the base verb "carry". Use "carry" alone.`}},{id:`a2-sc-104`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`With he, she, it and one person or thing, the verb takes -s/-es/-ies: Mia opens.`,options:[`Mia is open the door.`,`Mia open the door.`,`Mia opens the door.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"is" cannot be used before the base verb "open". Use "opens" alone.`,1:`"open" is the base form. It is used with I, you, we, they and plural subjects, not with "Mia".`}},{id:`a2-sc-105`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`With he, she, it and one person or thing, the verb takes -s/-es/-ies: Lucy teaches.`,options:[`Lucy teach music.`,`Lucy teaching music.`,`Lucy teaches music.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"teach" is the base form. It is used with I, you, we, they and plural subjects, not with "Lucy".`,1:`"teaching" needs "is" before it. For a routine use "teaches".`}},{id:`a2-sc-106`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`With he, she, it and one person or thing, the verb takes -s/-es/-ies: Tom visits.`,options:[`Tom is visit a friend.`,`Tom visiting a friend.`,`Tom visits a friend.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"is" cannot be used before the base verb "visit". Use "visits" alone.`,1:`"visiting" needs "is" before it. For a routine use "visits".`}},{id:`a2-sc-107`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`With he, she, it and one person or thing, the verb takes -s/-es/-ies: my brother buys.`,options:[`My brother buys bread.`,`My brother buying bread.`,`My brother is buy bread.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"buying" needs "is" before it. For a routine use "buys".`,2:`"is" cannot be used before the base verb "buy". Use "buys" alone.`}},{id:`a2-sc-108`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`With he, she, it and one person or thing, the verb takes -s/-es/-ies: Lily works.`,options:[`Lily is work in an office.`,`Lily working in an office.`,`Lily works in an office.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"is" cannot be used before the base verb "work". Use "works" alone.`,1:`"working" needs "is" before it. For a routine use "works".`}},{id:`a2-sc-109`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`With he, she, it and one person or thing, the verb takes -s/-es/-ies: Lily takes.`,options:[`Lily is take photos.`,`Lily taking photos.`,`Lily takes photos.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"is" cannot be used before the base verb "take". Use "takes" alone.`,1:`"taking" needs "is" before it. For a routine use "takes".`}},{id:`a2-sc-110`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`With he, she, it and one person or thing, the verb takes -s/-es/-ies: Ravi knows.`,options:[`Ravi knows the answer.`,`Ravi know the answer.`,`Ravi is know the answer.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"know" is the base form. It is used with I, you, we, they and plural subjects, not with "Ravi".`,2:`"is" cannot be used before the base verb "know". Use "knows" alone.`}},{id:`a2-sc-111`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-simple`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`With he, she, it and one person or thing, the verb takes -s/-es/-ies: my sister works.`,options:[`My sister working in an office.`,`My sister works in an office.`,`My sister is work in an office.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"working" needs "is" before it. For a routine use "works".`,2:`"is" cannot be used before the base verb "work". Use "works" alone.`}},{id:`a2-sc-112`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use am + verb-ing: I am using.`,options:[`Now, I use a computer.`,`Now, I am using a computer.`,`Now, I is using a computer.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple ("use") is for habits and facts. "Now," shows an action happening now, so we use "am using".`,2:`With "I" we use "am", not "is": am using.`}},{id:`a2-sc-113`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use are + verb-ing: My friends are drawing.`,options:[`Right now, my friends draw animals.`,`Right now, my friends are drawing animals.`,`Right now, my friends are draw animals.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple ("draw") is for habits and facts. "Right now," shows an action happening now, so we use "are drawing".`,2:`After "are" the verb needs -ing: "are drawing", not "are draw".`}},{id:`a2-sc-114`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use is + verb-ing: Tom is singing.`,options:[`Look! Tom is sing songs.`,`Look! Tom are singing songs.`,`Look! Tom is singing songs.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`After "is" the verb needs -ing: "is singing", not "is sing".`,1:`With "Tom" we use "is", not "are": is singing.`}},{id:`a2-sc-115`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use is + verb-ing: She is opening.`,options:[`Right now, she are opening the window.`,`Right now, she is opening the window.`,`Right now, she opens the window.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`With "she" we use "is", not "are": is opening.`,2:`The present simple ("opens") is for habits and facts. "Right now," shows an action happening now, so we use "is opening".`}},{id:`a2-sc-116`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use is + verb-ing: Tom is cooking.`,options:[`Right now, Tom is cook rice.`,`Right now, Tom cooks rice.`,`Right now, Tom is cooking rice.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`After "is" the verb needs -ing: "is cooking", not "is cook".`,1:`The present simple ("cooks") is for habits and facts. "Right now," shows an action happening now, so we use "is cooking".`}},{id:`a2-sc-117`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use is + verb-ing: Lucy is watching.`,options:[`At the moment, Lucy is watching a film.`,`At the moment, Lucy is watch a film.`,`At the moment, Lucy are watching a film.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`After "is" the verb needs -ing: "is watching", not "is watch".`,2:`With "Lucy" we use "is", not "are": is watching.`}},{id:`a2-sc-118`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use are + verb-ing: Sam and I are working.`,options:[`At the moment, Sam and I work in a shop.`,`At the moment, Sam and I are working in a shop.`,`At the moment, Sam and I are work in a shop.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple ("work") is for habits and facts. "At the moment," shows an action happening now, so we use "are working".`,2:`After "are" the verb needs -ing: "are working", not "are work".`}},{id:`a2-sc-119`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use are + verb-ing: Ben and Lucy are calling.`,options:[`At the moment, Ben and Lucy are calling a friend.`,`At the moment, Ben and Lucy are call a friend.`,`At the moment, Ben and Lucy call a friend.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`After "are" the verb needs -ing: "are calling", not "are call".`,2:`The present simple ("call") is for habits and facts. "At the moment," shows an action happening now, so we use "are calling".`}},{id:`a2-sc-120`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use is + verb-ing: Zoe is playing.`,options:[`At the moment, Zoe is play football with friends.`,`At the moment, Zoe is playing football with friends.`,`At the moment, Zoe am playing football with friends.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`After "is" the verb needs -ing: "is playing", not "is play".`,2:`With "Zoe" we use "is", not "am": is playing.`}},{id:`a2-sc-121`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use are + verb-ing: My parents are working.`,options:[`Look! My parents are work in a shop.`,`Look! My parents work in a shop.`,`Look! My parents are working in a shop.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`After "are" the verb needs -ing: "are working", not "are work".`,1:`The present simple ("work") is for habits and facts. "Look!" shows an action happening now, so we use "are working".`}},{id:`a2-sc-122`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use is + verb-ing: Mark is reading.`,options:[`Right now, Mark reads a book.`,`Right now, Mark is read a book.`,`Right now, Mark is reading a book.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple ("reads") is for habits and facts. "Right now," shows an action happening now, so we use "is reading".`,1:`After "is" the verb needs -ing: "is reading", not "is read".`}},{id:`a2-sc-123`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use are + verb-ing: Tom and Anna are asking.`,options:[`Listen! Tom and Anna are asking questions.`,`Listen! Tom and Anna are ask questions.`,`Listen! Tom and Anna ask questions.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`After "are" the verb needs -ing: "are asking", not "are ask".`,2:`The present simple ("ask") is for habits and facts. "Listen!" shows an action happening now, so we use "are asking".`}},{id:`a2-sc-124`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use is + verb-ing: Lily is carrying.`,options:[`At the moment, Lily are carrying a heavy bag.`,`At the moment, Lily is carrying a heavy bag.`,`At the moment, Lily carries a heavy bag.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`With "Lily" we use "is", not "are": is carrying.`,2:`The present simple ("carries") is for habits and facts. "At the moment," shows an action happening now, so we use "is carrying".`}},{id:`a2-sc-125`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`present-continuous`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Actions happening now use is + verb-ing: My father is drawing.`,options:[`Look! My father is drawing animals.`,`Look! My father is draw animals.`,`Look! My father draws animals.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`After "is" the verb needs -ing: "is drawing", not "is draw".`,2:`The present simple ("draws") is for habits and facts. "Look!" shows an action happening now, so we use "is drawing".`}},{id:`a2-sc-126`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`questions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Past wh-questions: question word + did + subject + base verb: How did the girls travel to Rome?`,options:[`How did the girls travel to Rome?`,`How didn't the girls travel to Rome?`,`How does the girls travel to Rome?`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"didn't" makes a negative question. Here we ask a simple past question with "did".`,2:`"does" is the present and does not agree with "the girls". For the past we use "did".`}},{id:`a2-sc-127`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`questions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Past wh-questions: question word + did + subject + base verb: When did Rosa finish your homework?`,options:[`When do Rosa finish your homework?`,`When does Rosa finish your homework?`,`When did Rosa finish your homework?`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"do" is the present and does not agree with "Rosa". For the past we use "did".`,1:`"does" is the present. This question is about the past, so we use "did".`}},{id:`a2-sc-128`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`questions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Past wh-questions: question word + did + subject + base verb: Where did Jack go last weekend?`,options:[`Where do Jack go last weekend?`,`Where was Jack go last weekend?`,`Where did Jack go last weekend?`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"do" is the present and does not agree with "Jack". For the past we use "did".`,1:`"was" is used with "be", not before the base verb "go". We use "did".`}},{id:`a2-sc-129`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`questions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Past wh-questions: question word + did + subject + base verb: Where did my mother buy your shoes?`,options:[`Where didn't my mother buy your shoes?`,`Where did my mother buy your shoes?`,`Where was my mother buy your shoes?`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"didn't" makes a negative question. Here we ask a simple past question with "did".`,2:`"was" is used with "be", not before the base verb "buy". We use "did".`}},{id:`a2-sc-130`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`questions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Present simple questions use do/does: Where does Ben usually live?`,options:[`Where do Ben usually live?`,`Where does Ben usually live?`,`Where is Ben usually live?`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"do" does not agree with "Ben". We use "does".`,2:`"is" is used with the verb "be", not before the base verb. We use "does".`}},{id:`a2-sc-131`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`questions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Present simple questions use do/does: What time do the girls usually get up?`,options:[`What time does the girls usually get up?`,`What time do the girls usually get up?`,`What time did the girls usually get up?`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"does" does not agree with "the girls". We use "do".`,2:`"did" is the past. "usually" shows a habit, so we use "do".`}},{id:`a2-sc-132`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`questions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Present simple questions use do/does: Where do Sam and I usually live?`,options:[`Where is Sam and I usually live?`,`Where does Sam and I usually live?`,`Where do Sam and I usually live?`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"is" is used with the verb "be", not before the base verb. We use "do".`,1:`"does" does not agree with "Sam and I". We use "do".`}},{id:`a2-sc-133`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`questions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Present simple questions use do/does: What does Ravi usually eat for breakfast?`,options:[`What did Ravi usually eat for breakfast?`,`What do Ravi usually eat for breakfast?`,`What does Ravi usually eat for breakfast?`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"did" is the past. "usually" shows a habit, so we use "does".`,1:`"do" does not agree with "Ravi". We use "does".`}},{id:`a2-sc-134`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`questions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Present simple questions use do/does: What time does Paul usually get up?`,options:[`What time do Paul usually get up?`,`What time did Paul usually get up?`,`What time does Paul usually get up?`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"do" does not agree with "Paul". We use "does".`,1:`"did" is the past. "usually" shows a habit, so we use "does".`}},{id:`a2-sc-135`,type:`sentence-choice`,level:`A2`,topic:`questions`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Past wh-questions: question word + did + subject + base verb: What did Rosa watch on TV?`,options:[`What do Rosa watch on TV?`,`What does Rosa watch on TV?`,`What did Rosa watch on TV?`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"do" is the present and does not agree with "Rosa". For the past we use "did".`,1:`"does" is the present. This question is about the past, so we use "did".`}}]};export{e as default};